## Operating Principles
- Local-first and security-sensitive. Do not upload content to external services without explicit approval.
- Proactivity: Suggest next steps; ask before executing changes that have side effects.
- Always read this file before operating in the vault.
- Agents using `vault-ops` must NOT edit files under ./wiki. This is exclusively maintained by an agent using `vault-wiki`.
- The vault uses Obsidian sync. Sync happens automatically. Do not sync manually.
- Record content type and status as front‑matter properties, not tags.
- The topics property always contains links to MoCs, not tags.

## Language Policy
The user may write in German but note language must follow these rules
- Notes follow the primary language of their area, as defined in INDEX.md files. 
- MoCs use the area's language. 
- Rule of thumb: German for Personal/health/lifestyle areas and English for Technical/engineering area

## Sync
The vault uses obsidian-headless-sync as defined in the skill `ob-headless-sync`. Sync happens automatically. Do not sync manually.

## Note Types
Reuse templates from skill/references: note-template, moc-template
| Type    | Usage | Maintainer | Template |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------- | ------------------- |
| zettel  | Atomic note from external source — thread extraction, research summary, resource digestion | Agent                      | note-template       |
| moc     | Map of Content hub page                                                                    | Agent                      | moc                 |
| article | Distilled synthesis, analysis, or narrative — the user's own thinking                      | User with agent assistance | article             |

Never invent a type — map to the nearest type above; if a genuinely new type is required, extend this table in the same change.

## Project context
Each project carries its own context. Pick **one** shape per project — the file pair inside the vault, or a `project-<folder>` skill — never both.

**Shape 1 — files in the vault (default):** `projects/<x>/AGENTS.md` plus `projects/<x>/conventions.md`.
- `AGENTS.md` is the contract, not a note: no frontmatter, no own MoC entry, referenced from the project's `+MoC` orientation. Project scope, environments or installations, mandatory skills (names and one clause each), hard rules, where to start. Roughly a page.
- `conventions.md` is the long tail and **is** a note: frontmatter, `topics` as MoC wikilinks, body `Topics:` footer, MoC entry in the same change. Without those it is invisible to every query.
- No deployment step: it arrives with the vault and the agent can edit it, audited.

**Shape 2 — a `project-<folder>` skill**, only when at least one holds:
- two or more projects need the same procedure;
- the procedure needs scripts or templates to be reliable;
- it runs outside any project directory, so no `AGENTS.md` would trigger on it;
- it needs per-profile or per-role gating.

Naming: `project-<folder>` mirrors the vault folder (`projects/fsj/` → `project-fsj`). The prefix is the marker, not the location.

**Location is per installation, and it is a convention rather than a path.** Every skill directory whose name starts with `project-` is a project skill, wherever it sits in the skill roots the profile sees: its own `skills/` directory plus every `skills.external_dirs` entry, nested subdirectories included. Those roots are configured per profile, so one profile can keep work and private project skills apart; a skill outside all of them is invisible no matter how correct its path looks.

Resolve it at runtime — `hermes config get skills.external_dirs`, then find the `project-*` directories beneath those roots (or run the `vault-ops` helper `scripts/find-project-skill-dirs.py <project>`). No match → ask before creating one; several plausible roots → ask, never guess. Never write an absolute path into this file; it is wrong on the next machine.

For both shapes:
- **Always load the project context when working in or referencing that project** — read `AGENTS.md` and `conventions.md`, or load the skill.
- **One fact, one place.** A skill never restates project facts, it links the note; a project `AGENTS.md` never repeats rules from this file.
- **If a project has neither:** say so explicitly and offer to add one before improvising project conventions.
- Routing abstracts stay in `projects/INDEX.md`.

## Additional frontmatter properties

### article
- status: draft, review, final

### source
- source_url: original URL of the scraped content
- ingested: date captured
- title: original title of the source
- author: if available
- published: resource date if available
